/* Verse Games: THE WHOLE COUNSEL · Nehemiah chapters 1-5
   Verse text is filled/verified from the KJV by tools/fill-verses.js */
VG.registerBibleChapters("nehemiah", [
{n:1, name:"Nehemiah's Prayer", seal:{ref:"Nehemiah 1:11",verse:"O Lord, I beseech thee, let now thine ear be attentive to the prayer of thy servant, and to the prayer of thy servants, who desire to fear thy name: and prosper, I pray thee, thy servant this day, and grant him mercy in the sight of this man. For I was the king’s cupbearer."}, facts:["Did you know? Nehemiah served as the king's cupbearer, one of the most trusted positions in Persia. The man who tasted the king's wine stood near him every single day.","Did you know? Nehemiah mourned and fasted for 'certain days' before he ever spoke to the king about Jerusalem. He planned in prayer long before he acted in public.","Did you know? Nehemiah's prayer fills most of chapter 1 and moves through worship, confession, Scripture, and request. It is one of the great prayers of the Old Testament."], questions:[
{q:"Who came from Judah and brought Nehemiah the report about Jerusalem?", options:["Hanani, one of his brethren","Tobiah the Ammonite","Geshem the Arabian","Sanballat the Horonite"], a:0, ref:"Nehemiah 1:2",verse:"That Hanani, one of my brethren, came, he and certain men of Judah; and I asked them concerning the Jews that had escaped, which were left of the captivity, and concerning Jerusalem.", insight:"Nehemiah had asked about his people before the bad news ever arrived. Caring enough to ask is where compassion begins."},
{q:"In which city was Nehemiah when he heard the news?", options:["Shushan the palace","Babylon the great city","Damascus of Syria","Samaria of Ephraim"], a:0, ref:"Nehemiah 1:1",verse:"The words of Nehemiah the son of Hachaliah. And it came to pass in the month Chisleu, in the twentieth year, as I was in Shushan the palace,", insight:"Nehemiah lived in comfort at Shushan, but his heart was in ruined Jerusalem. Comfort never stopped him from caring."},
{q:"What did the remnant report about the wall of Jerusalem?", options:["It was broken down","It had been fully rebuilt","It was guarded night and day","It was decorated with gold"], a:0, ref:"Nehemiah 1:3",verse:"And they said unto me, The remnant that are left of the captivity there in the province are in great affliction and reproach: the wall of Jerusalem also is broken down, and the gates thereof are burned with fire.", insight:"The broken wall was terrible news, and it became the doorway to Nehemiah's calling. God often starts His greatest works with our worst reports."},
{q:"What was Nehemiah's first response to the bad news?", options:["He sat down and wept, and mourned, fasted, and prayed","He asked the king for an army","He sent letters to the governors","He gathered timber at once"], a:0, ref:"Nehemiah 1:4",verse:"And it came to pass, when I heard these words, that I sat down and wept, and mourned certain days, and fasted, and prayed before the God of heaven,", insight:"Before Nehemiah lifted one stone, he fell to his knees. Prayer is the first work of every true builder."},
{q:"Whose son was Nehemiah?", options:["Hachaliah","Hilkiah","Hananiah","Hattush"], a:0, ref:"Nehemiah 1:1",verse:"The words of Nehemiah the son of Hachaliah. And it came to pass in the month Chisleu, in the twentieth year, as I was in Shushan the palace,", insight:"The Bible names Nehemiah's father though he never appears again. God records ordinary families, not only famous ones."},
{q:"How long did Nehemiah mourn and fast over Jerusalem?", options:["Certain days","Forty days and nights","Three full weeks","One whole year"], a:0, ref:"Nehemiah 1:4",verse:"And it came to pass, when I heard these words, that I sat down and wept, and mourned certain days, and fasted, and prayed before the God of heaven,", insight:"Nehemiah did not rush from tears to action. Some burdens need 'certain days' of prayer before we move."},
{q:"How does Nehemiah address God at the start of his prayer?", options:["O LORD God of heaven","O King of the whole earth","O Father of our fathers","O Mighty One of Jacob"], a:0, ref:"Nehemiah 1:5",verse:"And said, I beseech thee, O LORD God of heaven, the great and terrible God, that keepeth covenant and mercy for them that love him and observe his commandments:", insight:"Nehemiah prayed to the God of heaven, not the gods of Persia. True prayer begins with knowing exactly who God is."},
{q:"What does Nehemiah say God keeps for those who love Him?", options:["Covenant and mercy","Silver and gold","Palaces and gardens","Horses and chariots"], a:0, ref:"Nehemiah 1:5",verse:"And said, I beseech thee, O LORD God of heaven, the great and terrible God, that keepeth covenant and mercy for them that love him and observe his commandments:", insight:"Nehemiah anchored his prayer in God's promise, not his own goodness. Covenant and mercy are surer ground than our feelings."},
{q:"What does Nehemiah ask God to do with His ear?", options:["Let it be attentive to his prayer","Shut it to the cries of Israel","Turn it toward the king's palace","Listen to the songs of the Levites"], a:0, ref:"Nehemiah 1:6",verse:"Let thine ear now be attentive, and thine eyes open, that thou mayest hear the prayer of thy servant, which I pray before thee now, day and night, for the children of Israel thy servants, and confess the sins of the children of Israel, which we have sinned against thee: both I and my father’s house have sinned.", insight:"Nehemiah prayed day and night for his people. Persistent prayer is not bothering God; it is trusting Him."},
{q:"Whose sins does Nehemiah confess in his prayer?", options:["His own sins and his father's house","Only the sins of the king","Only the sins of the priests","The sins of the heathen nations"], a:0, ref:"Nehemiah 1:6",verse:"Let thine ear now be attentive, and thine eyes open, that thou mayest hear the prayer of thy servant, which I pray before thee now, day and night, for the children of Israel thy servants, and confess the sins of the children of Israel, which we have sinned against thee: both I and my father’s house have sinned.", insight:"Nehemiah said 'we have sinned,' not 'they have sinned.' Confession starts with us, not with blaming others."},
{q:"How does Nehemiah describe the way Israel had dealt with God?", options:["We have dealt very corruptly","We have served Thee faithfully","We have forgotten nothing","We have kept every law"], a:0, ref:"Nehemiah 1:7",verse:"We have dealt very corruptly against thee, and have not kept the commandments, nor the statutes, nor the judgments, which thou commandedst thy servant Moses.", insight:"Honest confession names the sin plainly. 'Very corruptly' is not self hatred; it is the truth that opens the door to mercy."},
{q:"Whose words does Nehemiah ask God to remember?", options:["Moses, His servant","Abraham, His friend","David, His king","Elijah, His prophet"], a:0, ref:"Nehemiah 1:8",verse:"Remember, I beseech thee, the word that thou commandedst thy servant Moses, saying, If ye transgress, I will scatter you abroad among the nations:", insight:"Nehemiah prayed Scripture back to God. He was not reminding a forgetful God; he was standing on God's own promise."},
{q:"According to the word Nehemiah quotes, what would God do if Israel transgressed?", options:["Scatter them abroad among the nations","Destroy them utterly at once","Send them back to Egypt","Shut the heavens over them"], a:0, ref:"Nehemiah 1:8",verse:"Remember, I beseech thee, the word that thou commandedst thy servant Moses, saying, If ye transgress, I will scatter you abroad among the nations:", insight:"Nehemiah owned the reason for the scattering before he claimed the promise of the gathering. Honest confession comes before confident asking."},
{q:"What does Nehemiah say God promised if Israel turned back to Him?", options:["He would gather them from the uttermost parts","He would give them the king's palace","He would destroy all their enemies","He would send them back to Egypt"], a:0, ref:"Nehemiah 1:9",verse:"But if ye turn unto me, and keep my commandments, and do them; though there were of you cast out unto the uttermost part of the heaven, yet will I gather them from thence, and will bring them unto the place that I have chosen to set my name there.", insight:"God's promise was bigger than Israel's scattering. No one is ever too far away for God to gather home."},
{q:"Where did God promise to bring His gathered people?", options:["To the place He had chosen to set His name","To the palace at Shushan","To the land of the Chaldeans","To the mountains of Samaria"], a:0, ref:"Nehemiah 1:9",verse:"But if ye turn unto me, and keep my commandments, and do them; though there were of you cast out unto the uttermost part of the heaven, yet will I gather them from thence, and will bring them unto the place that I have chosen to set my name there.", insight:"God did not just promise rescue; He promised a destination. His gathering always has a home at the end of it."},
{q:"How does Nehemiah describe the people in his prayer?", options:["Thy servants and Thy people, redeemed by Thy great power","Thy soldiers and captains, armed for battle","Thy priests and Levites, set apart for service","Thy strangers and guests, dwelling in tents"], a:0, ref:"Nehemiah 1:10",verse:"Now these are thy servants and thy people, whom thou hast redeemed by thy great power, and by thy strong hand.", insight:"Nehemiah pleaded redemption, not merit. 'Thou hast redeemed them' is still the ground of every prayer we pray."},
{q:"By what does Nehemiah say God redeemed His people?", options:["By Thy great power, and by Thy strong hand","By the king's decree, and by Persian gold","By the prophets' words, and by sacrifices","By their own strength, and by their armies"], a:0, ref:"Nehemiah 1:10",verse:"Now these are thy servants and thy people, whom thou hast redeemed by thy great power, and by thy strong hand.", insight:"Nehemiah grounded his plea in what God had already done. Remembering past redemption is the fuel of present faith."},
{q:"What office did Nehemiah hold in the king's court?", options:["The king's cupbearer","The king's scribe","The keeper of the forest","The captain of the guard"], a:0, ref:"Nehemiah 1:11",verse:"O Lord, I beseech thee, let now thine ear be attentive to the prayer of thy servant, and to the prayer of thy servants, who desire to fear thy name: and prosper, I pray thee, thy servant this day, and grant him mercy in the sight of this man. For I was the king’s cupbearer.", insight:"God used a cupbearer to rebuild a nation. Your ordinary job can be the platform for an extraordinary calling."},
{q:"What had Nehemiah asked about before he heard the bad news?", options:["The Jews that had escaped, and concerning Jerusalem","The price of corn in the market","The building of the king's palace","The wars of the Medes and Persians"], a:0, ref:"Nehemiah 1:2",verse:"That Hanani, one of my brethren, came, he and certain men of Judah; and I asked them concerning the Jews that had escaped, which were left of the captivity, and concerning Jerusalem.", insight:"Nehemiah asked about his people's welfare when he had no reason to. That kind of curiosity is the seed of compassion."},
{q:"What had happened to the gates of Jerusalem?", options:["They were burned with fire","They were shut with iron bars","They were guarded by soldiers","They were covered with gold"], a:0, ref:"Nehemiah 1:3",verse:"And they said unto me, The remnant that are left of the captivity there in the province are in great affliction and reproach: the wall of Jerusalem also is broken down, and the gates thereof are burned with fire.", insight:"Burned gates meant a defenseless city. Nehemiah faced the facts squarely, and facts told in prayer become fuel for faith."}
]},
{n:2, name:"The Night Survey", seal:{ref:"Nehemiah 2:20",verse:"Then answered I them, and said unto them, The God of heaven, he will prosper us; therefore we his servants will arise and build: but ye have no portion, nor right, nor memorial, in Jerusalem."}, facts:["Did you know? In Persia it was dangerous to look sad before the king, and Nehemiah had never done it before. His grief was so real that he risked his life just by letting it show.","Did you know? Between the king's question and his own answer, Nehemiah prayed a silent prayer to the God of heaven. Some of the most powerful prayers ever prayed take only a second.","Did you know? Nehemiah rode out alone at night with only the animal he rode on, telling no one his plan. He surveyed the whole ruin himself before asking anyone to help rebuild it."], questions:[
{q:"In which month did Nehemiah serve wine to the king in chapter 2?", options:["Nisan","Chisleu","Elul","Sebat"], a:0, ref:"Nehemiah 2:1",verse:"And it came to pass in the month Nisan, in the twentieth year of Artaxerxes the king, that wine was before him: and I took up the wine, and gave it unto the king. Now I had not been beforetime sad in his presence.", insight:"Chapter 1 happened in Chisleu and chapter 2 in Nisan, about four months later. God often answers 'certain days' of prayer in His own season."},
{q:"What did the king ask when he saw Nehemiah's face?", options:["Why is thy countenance sad, seeing thou art not sick?","Why hast thou come before me uncalled?","Why is the wine bitter in thy cup?","Why dost thou tremble in my presence?"], a:0, ref:"Nehemiah 2:2",verse:"Wherefore the king said unto me, Why is thy countenance sad, seeing thou art not sick? this is nothing else but sorrow of heart. Then I was very sore afraid,", insight:"The king read Nehemiah's face before Nehemiah said a word. People notice when our hearts are heavy, and honesty about it can open the door to help."},
{q:"How did Nehemiah feel when the king questioned him?", options:["He was very sore afraid","He was filled with boldness","He was overcome with laughter","He was calm and unbothered"], a:0, ref:"Nehemiah 2:2",verse:"Wherefore the king said unto me, Why is thy countenance sad, seeing thou art not sick? this is nothing else but sorrow of heart. Then I was very sore afraid,", insight:"Even with God's call on his life, Nehemiah felt fear. Courage is not the absence of fear; it is obedience in spite of it."},
{q:"Why did Nehemiah say his countenance was sad?", options:["The city of his fathers' sepulchres lieth waste","The king's enemies had invaded Persia","His brother Hanani had died","The harvest had failed in Judah"], a:0, ref:"Nehemiah 2:3",verse:"And said unto the king, Let the king live for ever: why should not my countenance be sad, when the city, the place of my fathers’ sepulchres, lieth waste, and the gates thereof are consumed with fire?", insight:"Nehemiah's grief was not about himself but about his people's shame. Godly sorrow over others' ruin is what moves us to act."},
{q:"What did Nehemiah do before answering the king?", options:["He prayed to the God of heaven","He bowed down to the ground","He called for the queen","He drank from the king's cup"], a:0, ref:"Nehemiah 2:4",verse:"Then the king said unto me, For what dost thou make request? So I prayed to the God of heaven.", insight:"Between the king's question and his answer, Nehemiah prayed. A second of prayer in a moment of pressure can steady a lifetime of work."},
{q:"What did Nehemiah ask the king to do?", options:["Send me unto Judah, that I may build it","Give me gold from the treasury","Make me governor of Samaria","Send soldiers to guard the palace"], a:0, ref:"Nehemiah 2:5",verse:"And I said unto the king, If it please the king, and if thy servant have found favour in thy sight, that thou wouldest send me unto Judah, unto the city of my fathers’ sepulchres, that I may build it.", insight:"Nehemiah asked for one clear thing: to go and build. Specific requests show real faith, and God honors them."},
{q:"Who was sitting beside the king when Nehemiah made his request?", options:["The queen","The high priest","The chief cupbearer","The captain of the guard"], a:0, ref:"Nehemiah 2:6",verse:"And the king said unto me, (the queen also sitting by him,) For how long shall thy journey be? and when wilt thou return? So it pleased the king to send me; and I set him a time.", insight:"The Bible notes the queen was present, a small detail that reminds us God arranges the very room where our requests are heard."},
{q:"What two questions did the king ask Nehemiah?", options:["How long shall thy journey be, and when wilt thou return?","Who will pay for the timber, and who will build?","Why dost thou weep, and what is thy sorrow?","Where is thy family, and who sent thee?"], a:0, ref:"Nehemiah 2:6",verse:"And the king said unto me, (the queen also sitting by him,) For how long shall thy journey be? and when wilt thou return? So it pleased the king to send me; and I set him a time.", insight:"The king wanted a plan, not just a passion. Faith that moves kings is faith with a timetable."},
{q:"From whom did Nehemiah request timber for the building?", options:["Asaph, the keeper of the king's forest","Hiram, the king of Tyre","Zerubbabel, the governor","Eliashib, the high priest"], a:0, ref:"Nehemiah 2:8",verse:"And a letter unto Asaph the keeper of the king’s forest, that he may give me timber to make beams for the gates of the palace which appertained to the house, and for the wall of the city, and for the house that I shall enter into. And the king granted me, according to the good hand of my God upon me.", insight:"Nehemiah knew exactly where the timber was and who held the key. Prayer does not replace planning; it powers it."},
{q:"What did Nehemiah credit for the king's favor toward him?", options:["The good hand of his God upon him","His own wisdom and eloquence","The queen's kind persuasion","The tribute paid by Judah"], a:0, ref:"Nehemiah 2:8",verse:"And a letter unto Asaph the keeper of the king’s forest, that he may give me timber to make beams for the gates of the palace which appertained to the house, and for the wall of the city, and for the house that I shall enter into. And the king granted me, according to the good hand of my God upon me.", insight:"Nehemiah saw God's hand behind the king's signature. Every open door has fingerprints on it, and they are God's."},
{q:"Who was grieved that Nehemiah had come to seek Israel's welfare?", options:["Sanballat the Horonite and Tobiah the Ammonite","The queen and her maidens","The priests and the Levites","The merchants of Jerusalem"], a:0, ref:"Nehemiah 2:10",verse:"When Sanballat the Horonite, and Tobiah the servant, the Ammonite, heard of it, it grieved them exceedingly that there was come a man to seek the welfare of the children of Israel.", insight:"Some people grieve when God's people prosper. Opposition often proves you are doing the very thing God sent you to do."},
{q:"How long was Nehemiah in Jerusalem before he rode out at night?", options:["Three days","Seven days","Forty days","One whole month"], a:0, ref:"Nehemiah 2:11",verse:"So I came to Jerusalem, and was there three days.", insight:"Nehemiah rested three days before he rode out at night. Even urgent work benefits from a pause to look and listen."},
{q:"What animal accompanied Nehemiah on his night ride?", options:["Only the beast that he rode upon","A caravan of camels","A chariot with horses","A mule laden with timber"], a:0, ref:"Nehemiah 2:12",verse:"And I arose in the night, I and some few men with me; neither told I any man what my God had put in my heart to do at Jerusalem: neither was there any beast with me, save the beast that I rode upon.", insight:"Nehemiah told no one and took no escort. Some surveys must be made quietly before plans are shared publicly."},
{q:"By which gate did Nehemiah go out by night?", options:["The gate of the valley","The sheep gate","The fish gate","The water gate"], a:0, ref:"Nehemiah 2:13",verse:"And I went out by night by the gate of the valley, even before the dragon well, and to the dung port, and viewed the walls of Jerusalem, which were broken down, and the gates thereof were consumed with fire.", insight:"Nehemiah inspected the damage himself instead of trusting reports. Leaders look with their own eyes."},
{q:"What problem did Nehemiah meet at the king's pool?", options:["There was no place for the beast to pass","The water had run completely dry","The gate was locked with iron","The pool was guarded by soldiers"], a:0, ref:"Nehemiah 2:14",verse:"Then I went on to the gate of the fountain, and to the king’s pool: but there was no place for the beast that was under me to pass.", insight:"The rubble was so deep his animal could not get through. Seeing the worst of it did not stop Nehemiah; it confirmed the need."},
{q:"Which way did Nehemiah go up to view the wall?", options:["By the brook","By the king's highway","Through the sheep gate","Along the city wall"], a:0, ref:"Nehemiah 2:15",verse:"Then went I up in the night by the brook, and viewed the wall, and turned back, and entered by the gate of the valley, and so returned.", insight:"Nehemiah rode the full circuit before he spoke a word. Thorough looking comes before bold leading."},
{q:"What did the rulers know about Nehemiah's night ride?", options:["They knew not whither he went, nor what he did","They followed him with torches","They had planned the route with him","They waited for him at the gate"], a:0, ref:"Nehemiah 2:16",verse:"And the rulers knew not whither I went, or what I did; neither had I as yet told it to the Jews, nor to the priests, nor to the nobles, nor to the rulers, nor to the rest that did the work.", insight:"Nehemiah kept his counsel until he had seen everything. Quiet preparation protects a plan until it is ready to be shared."},
{q:"What reason did Nehemiah give the people for building the wall?", options:["That we be no more a reproach","That the king might be pleased","That trade might increase","That the nations might fear us"], a:0, ref:"Nehemiah 2:17",verse:"Then said I unto them, Ye see the distress that we are in, how Jerusalem lieth waste, and the gates thereof are burned with fire: come, and let us build up the wall of Jerusalem, that we be no more a reproach.", insight:"Nehemiah tied the wall to God's honor, not just civic pride. Work done for God's name carries a deeper motivation."},
{q:"What two things did Nehemiah tell the people that strengthened their hands?", options:["The hand of his God upon him, and the king's words","The gold in the treasury, and the timber in the forest","The number of workers, and the plan of the wall","The names of the enemies, and their threats"], a:0, ref:"Nehemiah 2:18",verse:"Then I told them of the hand of my God which was good upon me; as also the king’s words that he had spoken unto me. And they said, Let us rise up and build. So they strengthened their hands for this good work.", insight:"Nehemiah gave them God's hand and the king's word: heaven's favor and earth's permission. People follow leaders who bring both."},
{q:"Who laughed the builders to scorn?", options:["Sanballat, Tobiah, and Geshem the Arabian","The rulers of Jerusalem","The priests of the temple","The merchants of Tyre"], a:0, ref:"Nehemiah 2:19",verse:"But when Sanballat the Horonite, and Tobiah the servant, the Ammonite, and Geshem the Arabian, heard it, they laughed us to scorn, and despised us, and said, What is this thing that ye do? will ye rebel against the king?", insight:"Mockery is often the first tax on obedience. The builders kept working while the mockers kept talking."}
]},
{n:3, name:"Builders of the Wall", seal:{ref:"Nehemiah 3:1",verse:"Then Eliashib the high priest rose up with his brethren the priests, and they builded the sheep gate; they sanctified it, and set up the doors of it; even unto the tower of Meah they sanctified it, unto the tower of Hananeel."}, facts:["Did you know? The high priest Eliashib is the first builder named, starting at the sheep gate near the temple. When leaders pick up tools first, everyone else follows.","Did you know? Shallum's daughters helped build the wall, one of the rare places in the Old Testament where women are named as builders. God's work has always had room for whole families.","Did you know? The nobles of Tekoa refused to work, but the ordinary Tekoites repaired two whole sections. Titles never built anything; willing hands did."], questions:[
{q:"Who built the sheep gate at the start of chapter 3?", options:["Eliashib the high priest with his brethren the priests","Nehemiah and his servants","The men of Jericho","The sons of Hassenaah"], a:0, ref:"Nehemiah 3:1",verse:"Then Eliashib the high priest rose up with his brethren the priests, and they builded the sheep gate; they sanctified it, and set up the doors of it; even unto the tower of Meah they sanctified it, unto the tower of Hananeel.", insight:"The high priest led the building, starting near the temple. Worship leaders who also work with their hands inspire everyone."},
{q:"Which builder is named as the son of Imri?", options:["Zaccur","Zadok","Zabbai","Zanoah"], a:0, ref:"Nehemiah 3:2",verse:"And next unto him builded the men of Jericho. And next to them builded Zaccur the son of Imri.", insight:"Zaccur is named only as 'the son of Imri,' yet his work is recorded forever. God remembers workers the world forgets."},
{q:"Who built next to the priests?", options:["The men of Jericho","The Tekoites","The Gibeonites","The merchants"], a:0, ref:"Nehemiah 3:2",verse:"And next unto him builded the men of Jericho. And next to them builded Zaccur the son of Imri.", insight:"Jericho's men came up to build Jerusalem's wall. God's work often draws help from unexpected places."},
{q:"Who built the fish gate?", options:["The sons of Hassenaah","The sons of Pahathmoab","The sons of Jericho","The sons of the apothecaries"], a:0, ref:"Nehemiah 3:3",verse:"But the fish gate did the sons of Hassenaah build, who also laid the beams thereof, and set up the doors thereof, the locks thereof, and the bars thereof.", insight:"Whole families took whole sections. When households serve together, the work moves faster and the bonds grow stronger."},
{q:"What did the builders of the fish gate set up?", options:["The beams, the doors, the locks, and the bars","The pillars, the curtains, and the veil","The altar, the laver, and the table","The towers, the trumpets, and the banners"], a:0, ref:"Nehemiah 3:3",verse:"But the fish gate did the sons of Hassenaah build, who also laid the beams thereof, and set up the doors thereof, the locks thereof, and the bars thereof.", insight:"They did not just raise walls; they hung doors with locks and bars. Good work finishes the job, down to the smallest detail."},
{q:"What did the Tekoites do that their nobles would not?", options:["They repaired the wall","They guarded the gates","They carried the timber","They fed the workers"], a:0, ref:"Nehemiah 3:5",verse:"And next unto them the Tekoites repaired; but their nobles put not their necks to the work of their Lord.", insight:"The nobles refused to put their necks to the work, but the common Tekoites built anyway. Faithfulness is never excused by other people's pride."},
{q:"How does the chapter describe the nobles of Tekoa?", options:["They put not their necks to the work of their Lord","They fled to the mountains","They mocked the builders","They demanded payment first"], a:0, ref:"Nehemiah 3:5",verse:"And next unto them the Tekoites repaired; but their nobles put not their necks to the work of their Lord.", insight:"Their names are remembered for what they refused to do. It is a sobering thought: we are remembered as much for our refusals as our deeds."},
{q:"Who repaired the old gate?", options:["Jehoiada the son of Paseah, and Meshullam the son of Besodeiah","Uzziel the goldsmith, and Hananiah the apothecary","Rephaiah the son of Hur, and Jedaiah the son of Harumaph","Malchijah the son of Harim, and Hashub the son of Pahathmoab"], a:0, ref:"Nehemiah 3:6",verse:"Moreover the old gate repaired Jehoiada the son of Paseah, and Meshullam the son of Besodeiah; they laid the beams thereof, and set up the doors thereof, and the locks thereof, and the bars thereof.", insight:"Even the old gate got careful repair with beams, doors, locks, and bars. In God's work, nothing is too old to be worth restoring."},
{q:"Which two tradesmen are named as wall builders?", options:["Uzziel the goldsmith and Hananiah the apothecary","Bezaleel the carpenter and Aholiab the engraver","The baker and the candlestick maker","The scribe and the moneychanger"], a:0, ref:"Nehemiah 3:8",verse:"Next unto him repaired Uzziel the son of Harhaiah, of the goldsmiths. Next unto him also repaired Hananiah the son of one of the apothecaries, and they fortified Jerusalem unto the broad wall.", insight:"Goldsmiths and perfume makers laid stones. Your trade does not disqualify you from God's building project; it equips you for it."},
{q:"Which tower did Malchijah and Hashub repair?", options:["The tower of the furnaces","The tower of Meah","The tower of Hananeel","The tower that lieth out"], a:0, ref:"Nehemiah 3:11",verse:"Malchijah the son of Harim, and Hashub the son of Pahath-moab, repaired the other piece, and the tower of the furnaces.", insight:"They repaired 'the other piece' plus the tower of the furnaces, taking more than their share. Generous workers always build beyond their assignment."},
{q:"Who built the wall together with his daughters?", options:["Shallum the son of Halohesh","Nehemiah the son of Azbuk","Baruch the son of Zabbai","Palal the son of Uzai"], a:0, ref:"Nehemiah 3:12",verse:"And next unto him repaired Shallum the son of Halohesh, the ruler of the half part of Jerusalem, he and his daughters.", insight:"Shallum brought his daughters to the wall. Faith is caught at home, and children who build with their parents remember it all their lives."},
{q:"How far did the valley gate repairs run?", options:["A thousand cubits, unto the dung gate","Five hundred cubits, unto the fish gate","Two thousand cubits, unto the water gate","Seven hundred cubits, unto the old gate"], a:0, ref:"Nehemiah 3:13",verse:"The valley gate repaired Hanun, and the inhabitants of Zanoah; they built it, and set up the doors thereof, the locks thereof, and the bars thereof, and a thousand cubits on the wall unto the dung gate.", insight:"A thousand cubits of wall, measured and recorded. God notices the length of our labor, and no faithful stretch goes unmarked."},
{q:"Who repaired the dung gate?", options:["Malchiah the son of Rechab","Hanun of Zanoah","Shallun the son of Colhozeh","Nehemiah the son of Azbuk"], a:0, ref:"Nehemiah 3:14",verse:"But the dung gate repaired Malchiah the son of Rechab, the ruler of part of Beth-haccerem; he build it, and set up the doors thereof, the locks thereof, and the bars thereof.", insight:"Someone had to repair the dung gate, the least glamorous gate of all. No assignment is beneath the dignity of God's servant."},
{q:"Who repaired the gate of the fountain?", options:["Shallun the son of Colhozeh","Malchiah the son of Rechab","Hanun, and the men of Zanoah","Baruch the son of Zabbai"], a:0, ref:"Nehemiah 3:15",verse:"But the gate of the fountain repaired Shallun the son of Col-hozeh, the ruler of part of Mizpah; he built it, and covered it, and set up the doors thereof, the locks thereof, and the bars thereof, and the wall of the pool of Siloah by the king’s garden, and unto the stairs that go down from the city of David.", insight:"Shallun built the gate, covered it, hung its doors, and repaired the wall of the pool besides. Thorough workers leave nothing half done."},
{q:"Which pool's wall is mentioned in verse 15?", options:["The pool of Siloah","The pool of Bethesda","The pool of Gibeon","The king's pool"], a:0, ref:"Nehemiah 3:15",verse:"But the gate of the fountain repaired Shallun the son of Col-hozeh, the ruler of part of Mizpah; he built it, and covered it, and set up the doors thereof, the locks thereof, and the bars thereof, and the wall of the pool of Siloah by the king’s garden, and unto the stairs that go down from the city of David.", insight:"The wall ran by the pool of Siloah, by the king's garden. God's builders worked in beautiful places and broken places alike."},
{q:"Which other Nehemiah repaired the wall?", options:["Nehemiah the son of Azbuk","Nehemiah the son of Hachaliah","Nehemiah the priest","Nehemiah the Levite"], a:0, ref:"Nehemiah 3:16",verse:"After him repaired Nehemiah the son of Azbuk, the ruler of the half part of Beth-zur, unto the place over against the sepulchres of David, and to the pool that was made, and unto the house of the mighty.", insight:"There were two Nehemiahs on that wall. God does not need famous names; He needs faithful ones, even ones who share yours."},
{q:"Who 'earnestly repaired' another piece of the wall?", options:["Baruch the son of Zabbai","Binnui the son of Henadad","Palal the son of Uzai","Pedaiah the son of Parosh"], a:0, ref:"Nehemiah 3:20",verse:"After him Baruch the son of Zabbai earnestly repaired the other piece, from the turning of the wall unto the door of the house of Eliashib the high priest.", insight:"Baruch repaired 'earnestly,' with zeal you could see. God loves workers whose hearts are in their hands' labor."},
{q:"Where did the Nethinims dwell as they repaired?", options:["In Ophel, by the water gate toward the east","In the city of David, by the stairs","In Bethzur, by the sepulchres","In Mizpah, by the fountain gate"], a:0, ref:"Nehemiah 3:26",verse:"Moreover the Nethinims dwelt in Ophel, unto the place over against the water gate toward the east, and the tower that lieth out.", insight:"The temple servants lived in Ophel and built near their home. Serve where you live; build where you dwell."},
{q:"From where did the priests repair the wall?", options:["From above the horse gate, every one over against his house","From the sheep gate unto the tower of Meah","From the valley gate unto the dung gate","From the old gate unto the broad wall"], a:0, ref:"Nehemiah 3:28",verse:"From above the horse gate repaired the priests, every one over against his house.", insight:"Each priest built in front of his own house. When everyone repairs what is nearest to them, the whole wall gets built."},
{q:"Who finished the circuit back at the sheep gate?", options:["The goldsmiths and the merchants","The priests and the Levites","The porters and the singers","The Nethinims and the Tekoites"], a:0, ref:"Nehemiah 3:32",verse:"And between the going up of the corner unto the sheep gate repaired the goldsmiths and the merchants.", insight:"The chapter that began with the high priest ends with goldsmiths and merchants. From the sanctuary to the marketplace, every class of people had a place on the wall."}
]},
{n:4, name:"Swords and Trowels", seal:{ref:"Nehemiah 4:20",verse:"In what place therefore ye hear the sound of the trumpet, resort ye thither unto us: our God shall fight for us."}, facts:["Did you know? Tobiah mocked that if a fox climbed on the wall, it would break down. The finished wall stood strong for centuries, and no fox ever managed it.","Did you know? Half the men held spears while the other half built, and every builder worked with his sword at his side. Prayer and preparation went together on that wall.","Did you know? When the wall reached half its height, the text says 'the people had a mind to work.' Willing hearts build faster than driven hands."], questions:[
{q:"What did Sanballat do when he heard that the wall was being built?", options:["He was wroth, took great indignation, and mocked the Jews","He sent gifts to encourage the builders","He asked to join the work","He wrote a letter of praise to the king"], a:0, ref:"Nehemiah 4:1",verse:"But it came to pass, that when Sanballat heard that we builded the wall, he was wroth, and took great indignation, and mocked the Jews.", insight:"Sanballat's anger was the compliment of an enemy. When God's work advances, those who hate it rarely stay quiet."},
{q:"Before whom did Sanballat mock the Jews?", options:["His brethren and the army of Samaria","The king and his counselors","The priests of Jerusalem","The merchants of Tyre"], a:0, ref:"Nehemiah 4:2",verse:"And he spake before his brethren and the army of Samaria, and said, What do these feeble Jews? will they fortify themselves? will they sacrifice? will they make an end in a day? will they revive the stones out of the heaps of the rubbish which are burned?", insight:"Sanballat performed his mockery before an audience. Ridicule loves a crowd, but it did not slow the builders for a moment."},
{q:"What did Tobiah the Ammonite say about the wall?", options:["If a fox go up, he shall even break down their stone wall","If the rain falls, it shall wash away","If the king hears, he shall tear it down","If the sun shines, the mortar shall melt"], a:0, ref:"Nehemiah 4:3",verse:"Now Tobiah the Ammonite was by him, and he said, Even that which they build, if a fox go up, he shall even break down their stone wall.", insight:"Tobiah's taunt was meant to make the work look small. Small beginnings in God's hands have a way of outlasting big mockery."},
{q:"How did Nehemiah answer the mockery?", options:["With prayer: 'Hear, O our God; for we are despised'","With an army of his own","By stopping the work for a month","By mocking Sanballat in return"], a:0, ref:"Nehemiah 4:4",verse:"Hear, O our God; for we are despised: and turn their reproach upon their own head, and give them for a prey in the land of captivity:", insight:"Nehemiah took the insults straight to God instead of answering them himself. The best reply to mockery is prayer, not retaliation."},
{q:"What bold thing did Nehemiah ask God to do to the mockers?", options:["Turn their reproach upon their own head","Strike them dead at once","Send fire from heaven upon them","Scatter them among the nations"], a:0, ref:"Nehemiah 4:5",verse:"And cover not their iniquity, and let not their sin be blotted out from before thee: for they have provoked thee to anger before the builders.", insight:"Nehemiah prayed boldly because the mockers had provoked God, not just him. When God's honor is at stake, bold prayers are fitting."},
{q:"How far had the wall risen when the enemies conspired?", options:["It was joined together unto the half thereof","It was finished to the very top","Only the foundation was laid","It had reached the third part"], a:0, ref:"Nehemiah 4:6",verse:"So built we the wall; and all the wall was joined together unto the half thereof: for the people had a mind to work.", insight:"Half built was enough to alarm the enemy. Progress, not perfection, is what threatens the plans of darkness."},
{q:"Why did the work move forward so quickly?", options:["The people had a mind to work","The king sent a thousand laborers","The enemies supplied the timber","The weather was unusually mild"], a:0, ref:"Nehemiah 4:6",verse:"So built we the wall; and all the wall was joined together unto the half thereof: for the people had a mind to work.", insight:"Willing hearts build faster than driven hands. 'A mind to work' is worth more than a multitude of workers."},
{q:"Who joined Sanballat and Tobiah when they heard the breaches were stopped?", options:["The Arabians, the Ammonites, and the Ashdodites","The Egyptians, the Moabites, and the Edomites","The Persians, the Medes, and the Elamites","The Greeks, the Romans, and the Syrians"], a:0, ref:"Nehemiah 4:7",verse:"But it came to pass, that when Sanballat, and Tobiah, and the Arabians, and the Ammonites, and the Ashdodites, heard that the walls of Jerusalem were made up, and that the breaches began to be stopped, then they were very wroth,", insight:"Success attracted a coalition of enemies. The more of God's work gets done, the more opposition organizes against it."},
{q:"What did the enemies conspire to do?", options:["Come and fight against Jerusalem, and hinder it","Send a petition to the king","Bribe the builders to stop","Burn the timber in the forest"], a:0, ref:"Nehemiah 4:8",verse:"And conspired all of them together to come and to fight against Jerusalem, and to hinder it.", insight:"The plan was not just to fight but to 'hinder.' The enemy's goal is rarely your destruction first; it is your distraction."},
{q:"What two things did the Jews do when they heard of the conspiracy?", options:["They made their prayer unto God, and set a watch day and night","They fled to the mountains, and hid in caves","They sent gifts to Sanballat, and sued for peace","They stopped the work, and waited a year"], a:0, ref:"Nehemiah 4:9",verse:"Nevertheless we made our prayer unto our God, and set a watch against them day and night, because of them.", insight:"Prayer and a watch, heaven and earth together. They trusted God and still posted guards; faith never cancels prudence."},
{q:"What did Judah say about the workers' strength?", options:["The strength of the bearers of burdens is decayed, and there is much rubbish","The workers are lazy, and the work is vain","The timber is rotten, and the stone is soft","The wall is crooked, and must come down"], a:0, ref:"Nehemiah 4:10",verse:"And Judah said, The strength of the bearers of burdens is decayed, and there is much rubbish; so that we are not able to build the wall.", insight:"Even among God's people, discouragement spoke up. Tired workers see rubbish; leaders must help them see the wall rising anyway."},
{q:"What was the enemies' secret plan?", options:["To come in the midst among them, and slay them, and cause the work to cease","To poison the wells of Jerusalem","To burn the gates in the night","To steal the workers' tools"], a:0, ref:"Nehemiah 4:11",verse:"And our adversaries said, They shall not know, neither see, till we come in the midst among them, and slay them, and cause the work to cease.", insight:"The enemy planned a surprise attack to stop the work. Knowing the plan let Nehemiah prepare; discernment is a form of protection."},
{q:"How many times did the Jews living near the enemy warn Nehemiah?", options:["Ten times","Three times","Seven times","Twelve times"], a:0, ref:"Nehemiah 4:12",verse:"And it came to pass, that when the Jews which dwelt by them came, they said unto us ten times, From all places whence ye shall return unto us they will be upon you.", insight:"Ten warnings from those closest to the danger. God often sends His alerts through the people nearest the threat."},
{q:"Where did Nehemiah station the armed men?", options:["In the lower places behind the wall","On the top of the temple","Inside the king's palace","At the gates of Samaria"], a:0, ref:"Nehemiah 4:13",verse:"Therefore set I in the lower places behind the wall, and on the higher places, I even set the people after their families with their swords, their spears, and their bows.", insight:"He placed guards at the lowest, weakest points. Wise leaders strengthen the vulnerable places first, not the strong ones."},
{q:"What did Nehemiah tell the people to remember?", options:["The Lord, which is great and terrible","The king, which is mighty and rich","The wall, which is high and strong","The enemy, which is near and fierce"], a:0, ref:"Nehemiah 4:14",verse:"And I looked, and rose up, and said unto the nobles, and to the rulers, and to the rest of the people, Be not ye afraid of them: remember the Lord, which is great and terrible, and fight for your brethren, your sons, and your daughters, your wives, and your houses.", insight:"'Remember the Lord' came before 'fight.' Courage flows from remembering who God is, not from forgetting what we fear."},
{q:"For whom did Nehemiah tell the people to fight?", options:["Their brethren, sons, daughters, wives, and houses","The king's honor and the empire's glory","The gold and silver of the temple","The trade routes and the markets"], a:0, ref:"Nehemiah 4:14",verse:"And I looked, and rose up, and said unto the nobles, and to the rulers, and to the rest of the people, Be not ye afraid of them: remember the Lord, which is great and terrible, and fight for your brethren, your sons, and your daughters, your wives, and your houses.", insight:"He named their families, not abstractions. People fight hardest for the faces they love, and Nehemiah knew it."},
{q:"What happened to the enemies' counsel?", options:["God brought it to nought","The king forbade it","The builders bribed them","The weather destroyed it"], a:0, ref:"Nehemiah 4:15",verse:"And it came to pass, when our enemies heard that it was known unto us, and God had brought their counsel to nought, that we returned all of us to the wall, every one unto his work.", insight:"God dismantled the conspiracy before a sword was drawn. The best victories are the battles God fights while we keep working."},
{q:"How were Nehemiah's servants divided for work and defense?", options:["Half wrought in the work, and half held the spears","All built by day, and all guarded by night","A third built, a third guarded, a third rested","Half prayed, and half carried burdens"], a:0, ref:"Nehemiah 4:16",verse:"And it came to pass from that time forth, that the half of my servants wrought in the work, and the other half of them held both the spears, the shields, and the bows, and the habergeons; and the rulers were behind all the house of Judah.", insight:"Half built and half stood guard, and the rulers backed them all. Shared labor and shared risk hold a people together."},
{q:"What did every builder hold while he worked?", options:["He wrought with one hand, and held a weapon with the other","He carried stones with both hands","He held a trowel and a plumbline","He bore a shield before his face"], a:0, ref:"Nehemiah 4:17",verse:"They which builded on the wall, and they that bare burdens, with those that laded, every one with one of his hands wrought in the work, and with the other hand held a weapon.", insight:"One hand for the trowel, one for the sword. God's builders work and watch at the same time; devotion and vigilance belong together."},
{q:"What were the people to do when they heard the trumpet?", options:["Resort thither, for our God shall fight for us","Flee to the temple for safety","Lay down their tools and pray","Sound their own trumpets in reply"], a:0, ref:"Nehemiah 4:20",verse:"In what place therefore ye hear the sound of the trumpet, resort ye thither unto us: our God shall fight for us.", insight:"The trumpet was their rallying call, and their confidence was this: 'our God shall fight for us.' When God fights, our gathering is our victory."}
]},
{n:5, name:"Justice for the Poor", seal:{ref:"Nehemiah 5:19",verse:"Think upon me, my God, for good, according to all that I have done for this people."}, facts:["Did you know? Nehemiah served as governor for twelve years and never once took the governor's food allowance. He fed 150 people at his own table every day instead.","Did you know? The 'hundredth part' Nehemiah ordered the nobles to restore was the monthly interest they had charged, about twelve percent a year. He made them give the profit back.","Did you know? Nehemiah shook out his garment as a living parable: God would shake out anyone who broke the promise to the poor. The whole congregation answered, 'Amen.'"], questions:[
{q:"Who raised the great cry that opens chapter 5?", options:["The people and their wives, against their brethren the Jews","The priests and the Levites, against the king","The nobles and rulers, against Nehemiah","The merchants, against the moneychangers"], a:0, ref:"Nehemiah 5:1",verse:"And there was a great cry of the people and of their wives against their brethren the Jews.", insight:"The cry came from within, from the poor against their own brethren. The hardest wrongs to face are the ones inside our own house."},
{q:"What had some of the people mortgaged to buy corn?", options:["Their lands, their vineyards, and their houses","Their gold, their silver, and their jewels","Their oxen, their sheep, and their asses","Their swords, their spears, and their bows"], a:0, ref:"Nehemiah 5:3",verse:"Some also there were that said, We have mortgaged our lands, vineyards, and houses, that we might buy corn, because of the dearth.", insight:"They had mortgaged their homes just to eat. Poverty that costs a family its land is poverty that cries out to God."},
{q:"Why had some borrowed money upon their lands and vineyards?", options:["For the king's tribute","For the building of the wall","For a journey to Persia","For the temple offerings"], a:0, ref:"Nehemiah 5:4",verse:"There were also that said, We have borrowed money for the king’s tribute, and that upon our lands and vineyards.", insight:"Taxes on top of hunger had driven them into debt. Nehemiah saw that the burden was systemic, not just personal."},
{q:"What had happened to the people's sons and daughters?", options:["They were brought into bondage as servants","They were sent to school in Persia","They were married to the nobles","They were taken to build the wall"], a:0, ref:"Nehemiah 5:5",verse:"Yet now our flesh is as the flesh of our brethren, our children as their children: and, lo, we bring into bondage our sons and our daughters to be servants, and some of our daughters are brought unto bondage already: neither is it in our power to redeem them; for other men have our lands and vineyards.", insight:"Children in bondage, parents powerless to redeem them. This was the grief that made Nehemiah very angry, and righteous anger has its place."},
{q:"How did Nehemiah feel when he heard their cry?", options:["He was very angry","He was deeply amused","He was mildly concerned","He was quietly indifferent"], a:0, ref:"Nehemiah 5:6",verse:"And I was very angry when I heard their cry and these words.", insight:"Nehemiah's anger was not a loss of control; it was love taking offense at injustice. Some things should make God's people angry."},
{q:"Whom did Nehemiah rebuke, and what was his charge?", options:["The nobles and rulers, for exacting usury of their brethren","The priests, for neglecting the temple","The builders, for working too slowly","The people, for complaining too loudly"], a:0, ref:"Nehemiah 5:7",verse:"Then I consulted with myself, and I rebuked the nobles, and the rulers, and said unto them, Ye exact usury, every one of his brother. And I set a great assembly against them.", insight:"Nehemiah confronted the powerful, not the poor. True reform starts at the top, where the power to oppress lives."},
{q:"What did Nehemiah set against the nobles and rulers?", options:["A great assembly","A band of soldiers","A court of judges","A wall of separation"], a:0, ref:"Nehemiah 5:7",verse:"Then I consulted with myself, and I rebuked the nobles, and the rulers, and said unto them, Ye exact usury, every one of his brother. And I set a great assembly against them.", insight:"He brought the charge into the open before all the people. Injustice hates daylight, and Nehemiah gave it plenty."},
{q:"What argument did Nehemiah use against selling their brethren?", options:["We have redeemed our brethren sold unto the heathen; will ye sell your brethren?","The king forbids it, and will punish you","The wall will fall if you continue","The prophets have cursed this practice"], a:0, ref:"Nehemiah 5:8",verse:"And I said unto them, We after our ability have redeemed our brethren the Jews, which were sold unto the heathen; and will ye even sell your brethren? or shall they be sold unto us? Then held they their peace, and found nothing to answer.", insight:"They had bought their brethren back from foreigners, only to enslave them at home. Nehemiah exposed the contradiction with one piercing question."},
{q:"What did Nehemiah say about walking in the fear of God?", options:["Ought ye not to walk in the fear of God because of the reproach of the heathen?","Only the priests need to walk in the fear of God","The fear of God belongs in the temple alone","The heathen know nothing of the fear of God"], a:0, ref:"Nehemiah 5:9",verse:"Also I said, It is not good that ye do: ought ye not to walk in the fear of our God because of the reproach of the heathen our enemies?", insight:"Their greed was giving God's enemies reason to mock. How we treat the poor preaches a sermon to the watching world."},
{q:"What did Nehemiah order restored 'even this day'?", options:["Their lands, their vineyards, their oliveyards, and their houses","Their gold, their silver, and their jewels","Their sons, their daughters, and their servants","Their oxen, their sheep, and their corn"], a:0, ref:"Nehemiah 5:11",verse:"Restore, I pray you, to them, even this day, their lands, their vineyards, their oliveyards, and their houses, also the hundredth part of the money, and of the corn, the wine, and the oil, that ye exact of them.", insight:"Restoration was to happen 'even this day,' not someday. Justice delayed is justice the poor cannot afford."},
{q:"What part of the money, corn, wine, and oil was to be restored?", options:["The hundredth part","The tenth part","The half part","The third part"], a:0, ref:"Nehemiah 5:11",verse:"Restore, I pray you, to them, even this day, their lands, their vineyards, their oliveyards, and their houses, also the hundredth part of the money, and of the corn, the wine, and the oil, that ye exact of them.", insight:"The hundredth part was the interest squeezed from the poor. Nehemiah made the nobles return the profit, not just the principal."},
{q:"How did the nobles answer Nehemiah's demand?", options:["We will restore them, and will require nothing of them","We cannot do this, for we are poor ourselves","We will consider it, and answer later","We refuse, and will appeal to the king"], a:0, ref:"Nehemiah 5:12",verse:"Then said they, We will restore them, and will require nothing of them; so will we do as thou sayest. Then I called the priests, and took an oath of them, that they should do according to this promise.", insight:"Confronted openly, they surrendered completely. Plain dealing with sin, in love, still produces repentance."},
{q:"What did Nehemiah do with his lap as a warning?", options:["He shook it, saying, So God shake out every man that performeth not this promise","He filled it with stones from the wall","He tore it as a sign of mourning","He spread it out to receive an offering"], a:0, ref:"Nehemiah 5:13",verse:"Also I shook my lap, and said, So God shake out every man from his house, and from his labour, that performeth not this promise, even thus be he shaken out, and emptied. And all the congregation said, Amen, and praised the LORD. And the people did according to this promise.", insight:"The shaken garment was an acted parable: break this promise and be emptied like this fold of cloth. Nehemiah preached with his hands as well as his mouth."},
{q:"How did the congregation answer Nehemiah's warning?", options:["Amen, and praised the LORD","They wept, and rent their clothes","They were silent, and went home","They murmured, and complained"], a:0, ref:"Nehemiah 5:13",verse:"Also I shook my lap, and said, So God shake out every man from his house, and from his labour, that performeth not this promise, even thus be he shaken out, and emptied. And all the congregation said, Amen, and praised the LORD. And the people did according to this promise.", insight:"'Amen, and praised the LORD': the people owned the promise with joy. Justice done God's way ends in worship, not resentment."},
{q:"How long did Nehemiah serve as governor of Judah?", options:["Twelve years, from the twentieth to the thirty second year of Artaxerxes","Seven years, from the seventh to the fourteenth year","Twenty years, from the first to the twentieth year","Forty years, like Moses in the wilderness"], a:0, ref:"Nehemiah 5:14",verse:"Moreover from the time that I was appointed to be their governor in the land of Judah, from the twentieth year even unto the two and thirtieth year of Artaxerxes the king, that is, twelve years, I and my brethren have not eaten the bread of the governor.", insight:"Twelve years of leadership, and the record he wanted kept was what he refused to take. Character is clearest in what a leader declines."},
{q:"What had Nehemiah and his brethren not eaten all those years?", options:["The bread of the governor","The meat from the king's table","The corn of the tribute","The fruit of the vineyards"], a:0, ref:"Nehemiah 5:14",verse:"Moreover from the time that I was appointed to be their governor in the land of Judah, from the twentieth year even unto the two and thirtieth year of Artaxerxes the king, that is, twelve years, I and my brethren have not eaten the bread of the governor.", insight:"He had a right to the governor's allowance and never used it. Rights surrendered for others' sake are among the noblest sacrifices."},
{q:"What had the former governors taken besides bread and wine?", options:["Forty shekels of silver","A hundred talents of gold","The firstfruits of the harvest","The houses of the poor"], a:0, ref:"Nehemiah 5:15",verse:"But the former governors that had been before me were chargeable unto the people, and had taken of them bread and wine, beside forty shekels of silver; yea, even their servants bare rule over the people: but so did not I, because of the fear of God.", insight:"Forty shekels of silver, plus bread and wine, squeezed from an already burdened people. Nehemiah named the old corruption plainly so the new way would shine."},
{q:"Why did Nehemiah refuse to live like the former governors?", options:["Because of the fear of God","Because he was already rich","Because the king forbade it","Because the people demanded it"], a:0, ref:"Nehemiah 5:15",verse:"But the former governors that had been before me were chargeable unto the people, and had taken of them bread and wine, beside forty shekels of silver; yea, even their servants bare rule over the people: but so did not I, because of the fear of God.", insight:"'Because of the fear of God' was his whole explanation. The fear of God is the best governor of a governor."},
{q:"How many ate at Nehemiah's table, besides guests from the nations?", options:["An hundred and fifty of the Jews and rulers","Fifty of the priests and Levites","Three hundred of the builders","Twelve of the nobles only"], a:0, ref:"Nehemiah 5:17",verse:"Moreover there were at my table an hundred and fifty of the Jews and rulers, beside those that came unto us from among the heathen that are about us.", insight:"He fed 150 people daily at his own expense while refusing the governor's allowance. Generosity and integrity lived in the same man."},
{q:"What was Nehemiah's closing prayer in the chapter?", options:["Think upon me, my God, for good","Remember my sins, and forgive me","Bless the work of my hands","Give me rest from my labor"], a:0, ref:"Nehemiah 5:19",verse:"Think upon me, my God, for good, according to all that I have done for this people.", insight:"After twelve years of self denial, his prayer was simple: 'Think upon me, my God, for good.' A clear conscience can ask God to remember with confidence."}
]}
]);
